                <pb facs="0518" n="514" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg514"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/13970: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bucknell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-09-28T15:00:00+01:00">September 28, 1938—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 28—11 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>245. I am reliably informed that in a secret meeting of the Council this morning
                    a resolution along the lines reported in my telegram 242, September 27, 5 p.m.,
                    was opposed by Belgium and Sweden on the ground that it was inconsistent with
                    their declared policies respecting article 16. The resolution has again been
                    referred to a <pb facs="0519" n="515" xml:id="pg515"/> drafting committee which
                    will endeavor to meet this objection. The Chinese delegate informs me that the
                    question is one of wording but not of substance.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bucknell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
